The Subirach’s Square was created by Josep Maria Subirach as part of the Passion Facade in the Sagrada Familia which was designed by Antoni Gaudi.
Gaudi was a world famous architect born on June 25 1852. Where he was born is disputed, mostly by him. He proclaimed he was born in Riudoms; however most of his official documents state he was born in Reus. Gaudi was a prominent artist in the Catalan Moderna art style, known for details, colorful facades, and its inspiration from Art Nouveau. Gaudi spent over 40 years designing the Sagrada Familia and dressed very modestly. When he died, he was crossing the street and hit by a tram but people did not connect the unconscious body to the famous architect, resulting in him getting care at a hospital that could not give him the cure he needed. When they finally figured it out, he was beyond saving. He was mathematically oriented and made many scales for his designs including for the Passion Facade.
The Passion Facade faces the west, towards the sunset, and is a depiction of Jesus Christ’s betrayal by Judas and Peter, his death, and resurrection. Specifically, the Subirach’s Square is in the section where Judas kisses Jesus, the signal to the guards to arrest Him. In it, Subirach added snakes by Juda’s feet to represent sin and behind them, the Subirach’s Square. All the rows, columns, and diagonals add up to 33, which is Jesus’s canonical age when he ascends to heaven.
Passion Facade was created in juxtaposition to the Nativity Facade, which is beautiful, lush, and soft. Facing the Nativity Facade, Gaudi wanted the Passion Facade "to inspire fear... and depict how bloody the sacrifice was," which is why Subirach, who was known for his dramatic and geometric art style, was chosen to take on this project. He, like Gaudi, spent a large part of his remaining life designing and working on it. Though he is now most famous for the sculpting and other contributions to the facade, he was already a well known sculptor in his own right, with over 700 works around Barcelona. When he agreed to being the artist on his project, he had two conditions. One, to replicate Gaudi’s living conditions and get an apartment close to the church and two, to be able to use his own art style. This was very controversial and still debated on whether it was within Gaudi’s original vision.
Pope Leo the 14th visited and blessed it on its unveiling on June 10th, 2026, precisely 100 years after Gaudi’s death and stands as the largest church in the world. The Sagrada Familia is in Barcelona and in addition to its spectacular height, beauty, and significance, it also has a lot of architectural novelties too. Most notably, through emulating nature, he was able to eliminate all straight lines and Gothic buttresses, reducing the amount of structural arches.
In conclusion, Subirach’s Magic Square is magical because all the rows, diagonals, and columns add up to 33 which is significant because that’s how old Jesus was when he ascended to heaven, and is found on the Passion Facade on the Sagrada Familia in Barcelona, Spain.

